  "image_name": "1aa29bfbe1cb0edc8a5f8645c87564e0.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic contrasts a healthy liver sinusoid with a fibrotic one and highlights how cell populations change during scarring. In the healthy state, the vessel-like sinusoid is lined by sinusoidal endothelial cells and contains red blood cells. Resident Kupffer cells sit along the lining and are shown as self-renewing, while circulating monocytes are present but not expanded. Below the sinusoid, hepatic stellate cells appear relatively quiescent.\n\nIn fibrosis, the sinusoidal environment looks altered and darker, and immune and stromal cell behavior shifts. Monocytes are recruited into the tissue and differentiate into macrophages, which then proliferate into a population labeled scar-associated macrophages. At the same time, stellate cells become activated and expand into scar-associated mesenchymal cells. Arrows indicate a linked process in which recruited immune cells and activated mesenchymal cells proliferate and contribute to scar production.\n\nOverall, the figure’s main message is that healthy liver maintenance depends on resident Kupffer cells and stable stellate cells, whereas fibrosis involves recruitment of monocytes, emergence of scar-associated macrophages, activation of mesenchymal cells, and progressive extracellular scar formation.",
